-- Source text: Interpretation Act 2005 (Ireland), Act No. 23 of 2005, as enacted, from the unofficial plain-text rendering No.-23-of-2005.txt.
-- (c) Government of Ireland. Oireachtas Copyright Material is reproduced with the permission of the Houses of the Oireachtas.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS.
-- Repeal and its effects, from the Interpretation Act 2005 (Ireland):
--   s 2(2)   an enactment that has been replaced, has expired, lapsed or otherwise ceased to have effect is deemed to have been repealed (for the purposes of this Act);
--   s 3(1)   the four Acts the Act repeals, and when;
--   s 3(2)   the savings for a meaning, and for another matter, that a repealed Act provided for in another enactment;
--   s 26     repeal and substitution, and repeal and re-enactment;
--   s 27     what a repeal does not do, and proceedings after a repeal.
--
-- WHEN A THING IS "BEFORE THE REPEAL".
-- The repealing enactment comes into operation at a moment (the end of a day, s 16(2) to (4)) or, for an Act that says nothing, on a day with no moment named (s 16(1)).
-- A thing done on a day is BEFORE the repeal when that whole day ends at or before the moment.
-- Where the repeal has no moment, a thing on the day the repeal comes into operation cannot be placed and the function refuses; a day before it is before; a day after it is not.
--
-- JUDGMENTS THE ACT LEAVES TO THE READER ("consistently with", "same idea", "of a continuing nature") are INPUTS.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ie-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `ie-operation-and-construction`

§ `Named refusals`

-- src:153 | Date of passing of Acts of Oireachtas. 15.—(1) The date of the passing of an Act of the Oireachtas is the date of the day on which the Bill for the Act is signed by the President.
-- src:155 | Commencement. 16.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), every provision of an Act comes into operation on the date of its passing.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 ss 15(1), 16(1) (src:153, src:155)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 16(1) names no moment within the date of passing, so whether a thing done on that day precedes the commencement is not answered` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 16(1) names no moment within the date of passing, so whether a thing done on that day precedes the commencement is not answered"

§ `A thing done before the repeal`

-- src:155 | Commencement. 16.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), every provision of an Act comes into operation on the date of its passing.
-- src:156 | (2) Where an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the passing of the Act and whether the day is named in the Act or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the Act or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
-- src:157 | (3) Subject to subsection (4), every provision of a statutory instrument comes into operation at the end of the day before the day on which the statutory instrument is made.
-- src:158 | (4) Where a statutory instrument or a provision of a statutory instrument is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the making of the statutory instrument and whether the day is named in the instrument or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the statutory instrument or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
-- src:230 | “midnight” means, in relation to a particular day, the point of time at which the day ends;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 ss 16(1)-(4) (src:155-158); Schedule Pt 1 "midnight" (src:230)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      facts IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a thing on` d `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` facts MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the moment the text fixes`
    WHEN JUST m   THEN d AT MOST m's `the day it ends`
    WHEN NOTHING  THEN
        BRANCH IF d LESS THAN c's `the day the text names`    THEN TRUE
               IF d GREATER THAN c's `the day the text names` THEN FALSE
               OTHERWISE `section 16(1) names no moment within the date of passing, so whether a thing done on that day precedes the commencement is not answered`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `the commencement of` facts

§ `Section 2(2): an enactment that has ceased to have effect is deemed repealed`

DECLARE `How an enactment stands` IS ONE OF
    `it has been replaced`
    `it has expired`
    `it has lapsed`
    `it has otherwise ceased to have effect`
    `it still has effect`

-- src:92 | (2) For the purposes of this Act, an enactment which has been replaced or has expired, lapsed or otherwise ceased to have effect is deemed to have been repealed.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 2(2) (src:92)
GIVEN s IS A `How an enactment stands`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`for the purposes of this Act the enactment is deemed to have been repealed` s MEANS
    CONSIDER s
    WHEN `it has been replaced`                      THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it has expired`                            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it has lapsed`                             THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it has otherwise ceased to have effect`    THEN TRUE
    WHEN `it still has effect`                       THEN FALSE

§ `Section 3(1): the Acts this Act repeals`

DECLARE `An Act` IS ONE OF
    `the Interpretation Act 1889`
    `the Interpretation Act 1923`
    `the Interpretation Act 1937`
    `the Interpretation (Amendment) Act 1993`
    `another Act`

-- src:93 | Repeals and savings. 3.—(1) The following Acts are repealed:
-- src:94 | (a) the Interpretation Act 1889;
-- src:95 | (b) the Interpretation Act 1923 ;
-- src:96 | (c) the Interpretation Act 1937 ;
-- src:97 | (d) the Interpretation (Amendment) Act 1993 .
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 3(1) (src:93-97)
GIVEN a IS A `An Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 3(1) lists the Act` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `the Interpretation Act 1889`                  THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Interpretation Act 1923`                  THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Interpretation Act 1937`                  THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Interpretation (Amendment) Act 1993`      THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another Act`                                  THEN FALSE

-- Section 3(1) is a provision of this Act, so it comes into operation when this Act does: at the end of 31 December 2005 (s 1(2), s 16(2)).
-- src:84 | (2) This Act comes into operation on 1 January 2006.
-- src:93 | Repeals and savings. 3.—(1) The following Acts are repealed:
-- src:156 | (2) Where an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the passing of the Act and whether the day is named in the Act or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the Act or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 3(1) (src:93); s 1(2) (src:84); s 16(2) (src:156)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      a IS A `An Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 3(1) has repealed the Act by the end of` d `, the Act being` a MEANS
    `section 3(1) lists the Act` a
    AND `the enactment` `the facts about this Act` `is in operation at the end of` d

§ `Section 3(2): savings for a meaning and for another matter`

DECLARE `Facts for the savings in section 3(2)` HAS
    `the repealed Act assigns a meaning to a word or expression in another enactment`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the repealed Act provides for another matter in another enactment`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `without that provision in this Act the other enactment would be changed in intent or become unclear or absurd`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `applying to the other enactment the corresponding provision of this Act would change it in intent or make it unclear or absurd` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:98 | (2) (a) The repeal by this Act of an Act which assigns a meaning to a word or expression in another enactment does not affect the meaning so assigned if—
-- src:99 | (i) in the absence of that meaning in this Act, or
-- src:100 | (ii) by the application to the other enactment of the meaning assigned by this Act to the same or a similar word or expression,
-- src:101 | the other enactment would be changed in intent or become unclear or absurd.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 3(2)(a) (src:98-101)
GIVEN f IS A `Facts for the savings in section 3(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not affect the meaning assigned` f MEANS
        f's `the repealed Act assigns a meaning to a word or expression in another enactment`
    AND (   f's `without that provision in this Act the other enactment would be changed in intent or become unclear or absurd`
         OR f's `applying to the other enactment the corresponding provision of this Act would change it in intent or make it unclear or absurd`)

-- src:102 | (b) The repeal by this Act of an Act which provides for any matter (other than a matter to which paragraph (a) relates) in another enactment does not affect the matter so provided for if—
-- src:103 | (i) in the absence of that matter being provided for in this Act, or
-- src:104 | (ii) by the application to the other enactment of a matter provided for by this Act which corresponds to a matter provided for in the repealed Act concerned,
-- src:105 | the other enactment would be changed in intent or become unclear or absurd.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 3(2)(b) (src:102-105)
GIVEN f IS A `Facts for the savings in section 3(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not affect the matter provided for` f MEANS
        f's `the repealed Act provides for another matter in another enactment`
    AND NOT f's `the repealed Act assigns a meaning to a word or expression in another enactment`
    AND (   f's `without that provision in this Act the other enactment would be changed in intent or become unclear or absurd`
         OR f's `applying to the other enactment the corresponding provision of this Act would change it in intent or make it unclear or absurd`)

§ `Section 26(1): repeal and substitution`

-- The repealed enactment continues in force until the substituted provisions come into operation.
-- It is in force throughout a day when that whole day ends at or before the moment the substituted provisions come into operation.
-- src:196 | Repeals and substitutions. 26.—(1) Where an enactment repeals another enactment and substitutes other provisions for the enactment so repealed, the enactment so repealed continues in force until the substituted provisions come into operation.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(1) (src:196)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      `the substituted provisions` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repealed enactment is in force throughout` d `, the substituted provisions being` `the substituted provisions` MEANS
    `a thing on` d `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the substituted provisions`

§ `Section 26(2): repeal and re-enactment`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:198 | (a) a person appointed under the former enactment shall continue to act for the remainder of the period for which the person was appointed as if appointed under the new enactment;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(a) (src:197-198)
GIVEN d                IS A DATE
      `the last day of the period for which the person was appointed` IS A DATE
      reenacted        IS A BOOLEAN
      appointed        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person appointed under the former enactment continues to act on` d `, the period for which the person was appointed ending on` `the last day of the period for which the person was appointed` `, the former enactment being repealed and re-enacted` reenacted `, the person having been appointed under it` appointed MEANS
        reenacted
    AND appointed
    AND d AT MOST `the last day of the period for which the person was appointed`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:199 | (b) a bond, guarantee or other security of a continuing nature given by a person under the former enactment remains in force, and data, books, papers, forms and things prepared or used under the former enactment may continue to be used as before the repeal;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(b) (src:197, src:199)
GIVEN reenacted IS A BOOLEAN
      `a bond, guarantee or other security of a continuing nature was given by a person under the former enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the security remains in force` reenacted `the security being` `a bond, guarantee or other security of a continuing nature was given by a person under the former enactment` MEANS
    reenacted AND `a bond, guarantee or other security of a continuing nature was given by a person under the former enactment`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:199 | (b) a bond, guarantee or other security of a continuing nature given by a person under the former enactment remains in force, and data, books, papers, forms and things prepared or used under the former enactment may continue to be used as before the repeal;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(b) (src:197, src:199)
GIVEN reenacted IS A BOOLEAN
      `the data, books, papers, forms and things were prepared or used under the former enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the data, books, papers, forms and things may continue to be used` reenacted `, the things being` `the data, books, papers, forms and things were prepared or used under the former enactment` MEANS
    reenacted AND `the data, books, papers, forms and things were prepared or used under the former enactment`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:200 | (c) proceedings taken under the former enactment may, subject to section 27 (1), be continued under and in conformity with the new enactment in so far as that may be done consistently with the new enactment;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(c) (src:197, src:200)
GIVEN reenacted IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings were taken under the former enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `continuing them is consistent with the new enactment`  IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 27(1) prevents the continuation`               IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the proceedings may be continued under the new enactment` reenacted `the proceedings were taken under the former enactment` `continuing them is consistent with the new enactment` `section 27(1) prevents the continuation` MEANS
        reenacted
    AND `the proceedings were taken under the former enactment`
    AND `continuing them is consistent with the new enactment`
    AND NOT `section 27(1) prevents the continuation`

DECLARE `What paragraph (d) does to a statutory instrument` IS ONE OF
    `paragraph (d) does not apply`
    `the instrument is deemed to have been made under the new provision and remains in force until it is repealed or otherwise ceases to have effect`
    `the instrument is deemed to have been made under the new provision only to the extent that it is not inconsistent with the new enactment`

-- src:84 | (2) This Act comes into operation on 1 January 2006.
-- src:201 | (d) if after the commencement of this Act—
-- src:202 | (i) any provision of a former enactment, that provided for the making of a statutory instrument, is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, as a new provision, and
-- src:203 | (ii) such statutory instrument is in force immediately before such repeal and re-enactment,
-- src:204 | then the statutory instrument shall be deemed to have been made under the new provision to the extent that it is not inconsistent with the new enactment, and remains in force until it is repealed or otherwise ceases to have effect;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(d) (src:201-204); s 1(2) (src:84)
GIVEN `the re-enacting enactment` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
      `the former provision provided for the making of a statutory instrument and is repealed and re-enacted as a new provision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the statutory instrument is in force immediately before the repeal and re-enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the instrument is not inconsistent with the new enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What paragraph (d) does to a statutory instrument`
`what paragraph (d) does to the instrument` `the re-enacting enactment` `the former provision provided for the making of a statutory instrument and is repealed and re-enacted as a new provision` `the statutory instrument is in force immediately before the repeal and re-enactment` `the instrument is not inconsistent with the new enactment` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the enactment comes into operation after the commencement of this Act` `the re-enacting enactment` THEN `paragraph (d) does not apply`
           IF NOT `the former provision provided for the making of a statutory instrument and is repealed and re-enacted as a new provision` THEN `paragraph (d) does not apply`
           IF NOT `the statutory instrument is in force immediately before the repeal and re-enactment` THEN `paragraph (d) does not apply`
           IF `the instrument is not inconsistent with the new enactment`
              THEN `the instrument is deemed to have been made under the new provision and remains in force until it is repealed or otherwise ceases to have effect`
           OTHERWISE `the instrument is deemed to have been made under the new provision only to the extent that it is not inconsistent with the new enactment`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:205 | (e) to the extent that the provisions of the new enactment express the same idea in a different form of words but are in substance the same as those of the former enactment, the idea in the new enactment shall not be taken to be different merely because a different form of words is used;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(e) (src:197, src:205)
GIVEN reenacted IS A BOOLEAN
      `the new provisions express the same idea in a different form of words` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they are in substance the same as those of the former enactment`        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the idea in the new enactment is not taken to be different merely because a different form of words is used` reenacted `the new provisions express the same idea in a different form of words` `they are in substance the same as those of the former enactment` MEANS
        reenacted
    AND `the new provisions express the same idea in a different form of words`
    AND `they are in substance the same as those of the former enactment`

DECLARE `How a reference to the former enactment is read` IS ONE OF
    `paragraph (f) does not apply`
    `as a reference to the provisions of the new enactment relating to the same subject-matter`
    `the former enactment is disregarded in so far as is necessary to maintain or give effect to the other enactment`

-- src:197 | (2) Where an enactment (“former enactment”) is repealed and re-enacted, with or without modification, by another enactment (“new enactment”), the following provisions apply:
-- src:206 | (f) a reference in any other enactment to the former enactment shall, with respect to a subsequent transaction, matter or thing, be read as a reference to the provisions of the new enactment relating to the same subject-matter as that of the former enactment, but where there are no provisions in the new enactment relating to the same subject-matter, the former enactment shall be disregarded in so far as is necessary to maintain or give effect to that other enactment.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 26(2)(f) (src:197, src:206)
GIVEN reenacted IS A BOOLEAN
      `the transaction, matter or thing is subsequent to the repeal and re-enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the new enactment has provisions relating to the same subject-matter`          IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `How a reference to the former enactment is read`
`how a reference in any other enactment to the former enactment is read` reenacted `the transaction, matter or thing is subsequent to the repeal and re-enactment` `the new enactment has provisions relating to the same subject-matter` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT reenacted THEN `paragraph (f) does not apply`
           IF NOT `the transaction, matter or thing is subsequent to the repeal and re-enactment` THEN `paragraph (f) does not apply`
           IF `the new enactment has provisions relating to the same subject-matter`
              THEN `as a reference to the provisions of the new enactment relating to the same subject-matter`
           OTHERWISE `the former enactment is disregarded in so far as is necessary to maintain or give effect to the other enactment`

§ `Section 27(1): what a repeal does not do`

DECLARE `Where a thing stood immediately before the repeal` IS ONE OF
    `it was in force or existing`
    `it was not in force`
    `it did not exist`

DECLARE `What paragraph (a) says of a thing` IS ONE OF
    `paragraph (a) does not apply to it`
    `the repeal does not revive it`

-- src:207 | Effect of repeal of enactment. 27.—(1) Where an enactment is repealed, the repeal does not—
-- src:208 | (a) revive anything not in force or not existing immediately before the repeal,
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(1)(a) (src:207-208)
GIVEN w IS A `Where a thing stood immediately before the repeal`
GIVETH A `What paragraph (a) says of a thing`
`what section 27(1)(a) says of a thing that` w MEANS
    CONSIDER w
    WHEN `it was in force or existing` THEN `paragraph (a) does not apply to it`
    WHEN `it was not in force`         THEN `the repeal does not revive it`
    WHEN `it did not exist`            THEN `the repeal does not revive it`

-- src:207 | Effect of repeal of enactment. 27.—(1) Where an enactment is repealed, the repeal does not—
-- src:209 | (b) affect the previous operation of the enactment or anything duly done or suffered under the enactment,
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(1)(b) (src:207, src:209)
GIVEN `the day it was done or suffered` IS A DATE
      `it was duly done or suffered under the enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the repealing enactment` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not affect a thing done or suffered on` `the day it was done or suffered` `, duly done or suffered under the enactment being` `it was duly done or suffered under the enactment` `, the repealing enactment being` `the repealing enactment` MEANS
        `it was duly done or suffered under the enactment`
    AND `a thing on` `the day it was done or suffered` `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the repealing enactment`

-- src:207 | Effect of repeal of enactment. 27.—(1) Where an enactment is repealed, the repeal does not—
-- src:210 | (c) affect any right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment,
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(1)(c) (src:207, src:210)
GIVEN `the day it was acquired, accrued or incurred` IS A DATE
      `it was acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the repealing enactment` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not affect a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred on` `the day it was acquired, accrued or incurred` `, under the enactment being` `it was acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment` `, the repealing enactment being` `the repealing enactment` MEANS
        `it was acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment`
    AND `a thing on` `the day it was acquired, accrued or incurred` `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the repealing enactment`

-- src:207 | Effect of repeal of enactment. 27.—(1) Where an enactment is repealed, the repeal does not—
-- src:211 | (d) affect any penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of any offence against or contravention of the enactment which was committed before the repeal, or
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(1)(d) (src:207, src:211)
GIVEN `the day the offence or contravention was committed` IS A DATE
      `it was an offence against or contravention of the enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the repealing enactment` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not affect a penalty, forfeiture or punishment for an offence or contravention committed on` `the day the offence or contravention was committed` `, the offence being against the enactment` `it was an offence against or contravention of the enactment` `, the repealing enactment being` `the repealing enactment` MEANS
        `it was an offence against or contravention of the enactment`
    AND `a thing on` `the day the offence or contravention was committed` `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the repealing enactment`

DECLARE `Facts about proceedings at a repeal` HAS
    `the day the proceedings began`                                                                       IS A DATE
    `the day the proceedings ended`                                                                       IS A MAYBE DATE
    `they are in respect of a right, privilege, obligation, liability, offence or contravention of the kind in (c) and (d)` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:207 | Effect of repeal of enactment. 27.—(1) Where an enactment is repealed, the repeal does not—
-- src:212 | (e) prejudice or affect any legal proceedings (civil or criminal) pending at the time of the repeal in respect of any such right, privilege, obligation, liability, offence or contravention.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(1)(e) (src:207, src:212)
GIVEN p IS A `Facts about proceedings at a repeal`
      `the repealing enactment` IS A `Facts about an enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal does not prejudice or affect the legal proceedings` p `, the repealing enactment being` `the repealing enactment` MEANS
        p's `they are in respect of a right, privilege, obligation, liability, offence or contravention of the kind in (c) and (d)`
    AND `a thing on` (p's `the day the proceedings began`) `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the repealing enactment`
    AND (CONSIDER p's `the day the proceedings ended`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
         WHEN JUST e  THEN NOT `a thing on` e `precedes the commencement of the repealing enactment` `the repealing enactment`)

§ `Section 27(2): proceedings and penalties after a repeal`

-- src:213 | (2) Where an enactment is repealed, any legal proceedings (civil or criminal) in respect of a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under, or an offence against or contravention of, the enactment may be instituted, continued or enforced, and any penalty, forfeiture or punishment in respect of such offence or contravention may be imposed and carried out, as if the enactment had not been repealed.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 27(2) (src:213)
GIVEN `the enactment has been repealed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings or the penalty relate to a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment, or to an offence against or contravention of it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the proceedings may be instituted, continued or enforced, and the penalty imposed and carried out, as if the enactment had not been repealed` `the enactment has been repealed` `the proceedings or the penalty relate to a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment, or to an offence against or contravention of it` MEANS
    `the enactment has been repealed`
    AND `the proceedings or the penalty relate to a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under the enactment, or to an offence against or contravention of it`
